// TERMINAL VELOCITY — LAST TRAIN — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// TERMINAL VELOCITY as 80s jangle pop falling into dream pop — a game alternate of neon,
// selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// LAST TRAIN: the last train home through the night city. A Rickenbacker 12-string picks the
// Seseragi figure's chords the way Johnny Marr would; at the minor turn the band drives into the
// night like "Just Like Heaven" (150 BPM — TERMINAL VELOCITY's own tempo); at the second strike
// it falls asleep against the window into Cocteau Twins / Slowdive shimmer, and wakes a whole
// step up for the last stretch home. Japanese city-at-night guitar pop by way of Manchester.
// 
// THE TUNE is neon's, note for note: the Seseragi figure, its minor turn and cadence, the second
// theme (neon bars 29–36), the Shibuya melody and the ostinato (as a bass line), and neon's sweep.
// Re-harmonised where jangle pop wants it — at dusk | C6/9 | Am11 | Gadd9 | Em11 | Fmaj9 | C6/9 |
// (I–vi–V–iii–IV–I under the same figure), at night | Cm(♭6) | A♭maj9 | G7♭9 | G7♭9 | Fm9 | A♭maj9 |.
// 
// THE LANDMARKS are neon's: 150 BPM, the cabinet screen loops bars 3–6, the loop returns to bar 15.
// Bar 15 (beat 56, the minor turn and the lightning) — the band stops after bar 14's second beat,
// a tom fill, and hits C minor with a crash, the Hook bass's entrance and the 12-string's first minor
// chord. Bar 45 (beat 176, the second strike) — the band stops after bar 44's second beat and lands
// on a crash, a low C, a raked CmMaj9 and the strings: the dream. The turn lands 12.8–17.6 s after
// the cabinet screen, as it does under neon's own song.
// 
//   1–2    THE CHIME — the 12-string rakes neon's sweep (its octave strings at neon's pitch); tom count-in
//   3–8    DUSK — the figure on the clean electric; the 12-string rolls the anchor shapes (D4 and G4
//          held on the top strings while the bass moves), the steel acoustic sparkles; sidestick
//          (the cabinet screen loops 3–6)
//   9–14   DUSK, the band — snare, tambourine, the 12-string pinching, the steel in off-sixteenths;
//          bar 14 stops after beat two over a tom fill
//   15–20  THE TURN (beat 56) — C minor; the Hook bass enters on root-fifth-octave; the drive groove
//   21–28  NIGHT LINE — neon's ostinato as a Hook bass melody, two octaves down; the electric
//          shimmer in 3-3-2 sixteenths; the figure returns over A♭
//   29–36  THE SECOND THEME — the chorus: Hook counter-line, bowed strings, open hats
//   37–40  UNDER THE TRACKS — the 12-string plays the hook itself over a tom groove
//   41–44  the figure and cadence again, building; bar 44 stops after beat two
//   45–52  THE DREAM (beat 176) — the Shibuya melody on the shimmer guitar in delay and reverb,
//          half-time drums, bowed strings and the Burnt Horizon pad (neon's own); the 12-string
//          picks slowly the second time
//   53–56  THE BUILD — the melody doubled, the drums a gear a bar, the riser, bass octaves
//   57–64  THE LAST STRETCH — D minor, the figure a whole step up
//   65–80  THE SECOND THEME in D minor, twice; the second time the shimmer doubles it an octave
//          down and the steel acoustic sparkles on top
//   81–84  HOME — the figure, the sweep a step up (neon's bar 83), G9 and the lead climbing into
//          the loop's C minor at bar 15
// 
// Guitars: FRPP-6 throughout. The 12-string (frppTwelveString, left) picks eighth-note rolls,
// pinches and cascades on open shapes — every note on a string of its own, so the shape rings —
// chosen bar by bar as the first real shape that rubs against nothing (its octave strings
// counted). Guitar 2 (right) is the steel acoustic Shimmer at dusk and the clean electric Shimmer
// at night, on the off-sixteenths and in 3-3-2 against the 12-string's eighths. No strums: the
// only struck chords are the two rakes, down. Lead: frppCleanElectric through chorus and exciter
// into the dotted-eighth echo; dream lead: frppCleanElectricShimmer with its own long echo.
// Bass: frppBassNeck twice — a Peter Hook line high on the neck through a chorus, and the roots
// in eighths underneath. Bowed strings: frppStringSection. Drums: the Breakbeat kick under the
// Eighties one, the Eighties gated snare and hats, breakbeat ghosts, tambourine, sidestick, toms.
// 
// Levels measured (solo LUFS through the master chain, _remix-bounce --lanes --range), against the lead:
//   chorus 29–36   kick +0.3, roots −1.4, snare −2.8, Hook bass −4.9, 12-string −6.2, strings −8.4,
//                  electric shimmer −9.0, hats and tambourine 12–16 under
//   dusk 3–14      12-string −4 (the jangle is half the hook here), steel shimmer −6.6, sidestick −10
//   dream 45–52    against the dream lead: snare −4, strings −8, 12-string −9, pad −11; the paper kick
//   build 55–56    the riser 7.7 dB under the lead
//   chorus 73–80   the shimmer octave-down double 4.6 under the lead
// Trailing master gain -6.3 dB after a tilt (−1.5 dB at 420 Hz, +2.5 dB shelf at 7 kHz) and mbCompN:
// −21.0 LUFS integrated, peak −4.8 dBFS; bands against the cabinet median: low-mid +3, mid +4, presence +3
// (a guitar band with bright leads), sub, bass and air inside ±2.
// 84 bars at 150, 134 s; the loop 15–84 is 112 s.
// Written by work/local/_neon-remix-last-train.mjs through work/local/_remix-lib.mjs, which
// self-checks chord tones on the beats, semitone clashes against everything sounding (the
// 12-string's octaves counted), one note per string in real shapes, strum parity, the loop wrap
// and neon's landmarks; re-running it rewrites this file. The Seseragi and Shibuya melodies are
// JR East's — the clearing caveat on neon.js carries over to this file unchanged.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
